{
  "example": "For `impl Thing { fn run() {} }` on one line, `habeas add-input --for-requirement requirements.foo --rust-item src/lib.rs:1` adds separate inputs for `impl[Thing]` and `impl[Thing]/fn[run]`. Both contain the complete raw line. {cite}`requirements.cli.add_input_rust_item`",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "The example's scenario is only partly asserted by its single `{test}` reference.\n\n**What the test covers.** `add_input_modes_capture_raw_sources_and_refresh_idempotently` (tests/commands.rs, marked at line 779) is the only `{test}` reference. It puts `fn main() { fn nested() {} }` on one line, src/main.rs line 4. It runs `add-input --rust-item src/main.rs:4`. It then asserts that:\n- stdout reports both `fn[main]` and `fn[main]/fn[nested]`;\n- the evidence file has exactly 3 inputs (the file-range input plus two Rust-item inputs);\n- a rerun is \"unchanged\" and leaves the file byte-identical;\n- `inputs[1].text` is the full raw lines of the `main` item (doc, attribute, citation comment, and the one-line fn).\n\nThis is a close analogue of the `impl Thing { fn run() {} }` example. It uses nested items on one line rather than `impl` plus `fn`. That substitution is acceptable. The parent/child selector form is the same.\n\n**Gap.** The example's expected outcome is that separate inputs exist for both selectors and that both contain the complete raw line. The test never inspects `inputs[2]`. That is the input for `fn[main]/fn[nested]`.\n- It does not check that input's stored `rust_item` selector in the evidence JSON. The selector is only seen in stdout.\n- It does not check that input's `text`, which should be the complete line 4.\n\nThe test therefore does not verify the \"both contain the complete raw line\" half of the example. A regression that stored an empty or truncated text for the nested or child input would pass this test. No other test, comment or FIXME covers or acknowledges the gap. `src/add_input.rs` and `src/rust_items.rs` appear to implement the behavior, but the example needs test assertions that cover its expected outcomes.",
